[SetUp "1"]

{ Does a4 stop ...b5 permanently or only make it harder? What supports an eventual e5 break? Name the structure and one resource for the other side. ANSWER: White's d5 pawn cramps Black. Black has a queenside majority and a potentially weak d6 pawn, compensated by active diagonals and files. Restrain ...b5 when useful, develop, and prepare e4-e5. Watch whether exchanging a key bishop would weaken your control of central squares. Black: Black seeks ...b5, ...Re8 and pressure against the center. The d6 pawn is rarely won by a single unsupported attack. } *

